      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Differences in Medicaid vs Medicare vs Private vs Self-Pay duodenal switch (DS) results are unknown. This study identified DS outcomes variations by health insurance."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Data from 1,681 DS patients were analyzed retrospectively: Medicaid (n = 138), Medicare (n = 313), Private insurance (n = 1,171), and Self-Pay (n = 59). General linear models included baseline and postoperative data and were modified for dichotomous variables."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Hypertension, obstructive sleep apnea, abdominal hernia, diabetes, and 9 other hepatobiliary, and somatic conditions were lowest in Private (P < .05). Self-Pay cholelithiasis, gastroesophageal reflux disease, back and/or musculoskeletal pain, and 3 others were lowest; asthma, angina, congestive heart failure, alcohol use, liver disease, and 3 others were highest (P < .05). Medicare had highest abdominal hernia and musculoskeletal pain, pseudotumor cerebri; lowest asthma, and polycystic ovarian syndrome (P < .05). Medicaid hypertension, sleep apnea, cholelithiasis, gastroesophageal reflux disease, diabetes, back pain, and 5 others were highest (P < .05); dyslipidemia and alcohol use were lowest."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Outcomes after DS vary by health insurance. These findings may facilitate management of DS patients."
          }
        ]
      },
